  "title": "Japan and Europe Joined Trump’s Push on Factory Overcapacity. Now They’re Fighting His Tariff Case.",
  "deck": "Tokyo and Brussels agreed to work with Washington in October. Both dispute the U.S. case for putting tariffs on their exports, while Beijing rejects the broader premise.",
  "category": "economy",
  "categoryLabel": "Economy & Energy",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-26",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-26",
  "body": [
    "In October, Japan and the European Union joined the United States in pledging to tackle excess manufacturing capacity. Seven weeks later, Washington cited that same problem as grounds for proposed tariffs on Japanese and European goods. Tokyo protested to Washington, Brussels rejected the finding against Europe, and Beijing dismissed the broader premise.",
    "The October statement called for governments to work together in specific industries where state support and other nonmarket practices can sustain more production than buyers can absorb. Japan, the EU, South Korea and India were among the signatories. All four appear on the list of 14 economies covered by the U.S. findings announced Wednesday.",
    "## What Tokyo, Brussels and Beijing are disputing",
    "Japan’s chief cabinet secretary, Minoru Kihara, said Thursday that Tokyo had conveyed its protest to Washington. He disputed the U.S. finding that Japan has the nonmarket policies or structural excess production capacity cited in the investigation.",
    "European officials say excess capacity is a genuine global problem and that much of it is concentrated outside the EU. At a trade meeting in Vietnam on Thursday, EU trade commissioner Maroš Šefčovič rejected the U.S. finding against Europe and called on Washington to honor the 15% rate the two sides had agreed to.",
    "China’s Foreign Ministry took a broader position. Spokesperson Guo Jiakun dismissed industrial overcapacity as a pretext for trade pressure and called for resolving disputes through talks. Beijing’s objection reaches the premise of the investigation, while Brussels’ objection centers on Washington applying that finding to Europe.",
    "## The rates tell only part of the story",
    "U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer concluded that policies in the 14 economies burden or restrict U.S. commerce under Section 301. For Japan, the proposal calls for tariffs of up to 15%, accounting for ordinary and existing Section 301 duties, with product exemptions. The EU faces a proposed 15% rate under one scenario, and China faces a proposed 20% rate. The treatment of existing duties and exemptions varies by economy and product.",
    "The October pledge left governments room to decide where overcapacity exists and what to do about it. Washington has now made its own findings under U.S. trade law. That leaves a specific question for the countries that signed the pledge. Can they still work together in the industries they agreed to examine while they contest Washington’s findings about their own factories?",
    "Written comments are due Jan. 5, 2027, and USTR has scheduled hearings to begin Jan. 25. The filings will show where Japan and the EU challenge Washington’s evidence and where they seek changes to the proposed duties."
  ],
